messages:
sam: heads up — i'm working on splitting infra/modules/service into service-v2 + networking-v2. ran a plan and there are duplicated provider blocks and two different provider versions in submodules. question: do we hoist provider config and version pins to the root? also how should we wire KMS secrets into the new module (pass ARN vs data lookup)?

ana: +1 on hoisting providers to the root. keeps provider constraints consistent and avoids terraform trying to install multiple provider versions at once. we should keep provider version constraints in the root module's required_providers and rely on terraform.lock.hcl for the exact mirrors. after you update required_providers run: `terraform init -upgrade` then `terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64` to refresh the lockfile.

mike: quick note about aliases — if any submodule used `provider = aws.assume` you'll need to declare `provider \"aws\" { alias = \"assume\" }` in the root and pass `providers = { aws = aws.assume }` into the module (or use `providers = { aws = aws }` depending on what the module expects). failing to do that is a common source of plan diffs.

sam: thanks. example of the root provider snippet i'm planning to use:```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = \"hashicorp/aws\"
      version = \"~> 5.0\"
    }
  }
}

provider \"aws\" {
  region = var.aws_region
}

provider \"aws\" {
  alias  = \"assume\"
  region = var.aws_region
  assume_role {
    role_arn = var.cross_account_role_arn
  }
}
```

rachel: for secrets wiring i'd prefer passing the KMS key ARN into the module (explicit) rather than letting the module perform a data lookup. makes testing and IAM tracing easier. so module input var.kms_key_arn string. if you need a fallback you can have `var.kms_key_arn = null` and let the module do a lookup only when null.

ana: +1 to the explicit ARN pattern. also update the module docs and the example usage under /examples/ to show both patterns.

sam: about lockfile: current submodules each have a terraform.lock.hcl from their dev workspaces. should i delete submodule lockfiles and keep only root terraform.lock.hcl in the repo root?

mike: yes, we keep a single lockfile at the repo root for our mono-repo terraform layout. remove child lockfiles (they're noise). after hoisting providers, run `terraform init` at root and commit the generated terraform.lock.hcl.

sam: got it. is there any rollout strategy that avoids a double-apply across modules? i want to split without downtime.

ana: you can do staged deploys: (1) deploy new root provider + lockfile (no infra changes) -> run `terraform plan` ensure no diffs; (2) apply; (3) update modules to v2 references and apply per-module targeting (eg `terraform apply -target=module.service_v2`) to create replacement resources while keeping old ones until verification; (4) cut traffic / switch references; (5) destroy old module. testing in staging first.

sam: perfect. i'll open a PR with the provider hoist + updated README and remove child lockfiles. pinging reviewers: @ana @mike @rachel for quick look. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/492 :eyes:

deploy-bot: build status for PR 492 — checks passed (lint, fmt, tfvalidate), plan uploaded: https://ci.internal/redwood/492/plan

mike: i'll take the review after lunch. if anyone wants to run the plan locally, run `TF_CLI_ARGS_plan=\"-input=false\" terraform plan -out=pr492.plan` and inspect with `terraform show -json pr492.plan | jq .`

rachel: small nit — when you remove child lockfiles, add a short note in the changelog entry so we don't accidentally resurrect them in future module PRs.

sam: will do. thanks everyone :rocket:
